<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yugra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yugra (Arabic Yura) was the medieval name for the lands and peoples of the far north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-russia">European Russia</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, around the northern Urals and the lower Ob. Arab writers describe the Yura as a people beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisu">Wisu</a> who sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> merchants, and from the eleventh century Novgorod sent expeditions there for tribute. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanty">Khanty</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansi">Mansi</a> are usually seen as their descendants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yugra.jpg" alt="Yugra" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jyrg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_yugra.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Russian chroniclers of the 12th to 17th centuries used Yugra, or Yugor Land, as a collective name for the lands and peoples east of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-mountains">Ural Mountains</a>, then inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanty">Khanty</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansi">Mansi</a>. In the early 16th century scholars such as Maciej Miechowita noted its likeness to ugry, an Old Russian name for the Hungarians, and the name of the Ugric languages rests on that assumption. The link between the two words is disputed: András Róna-Tas has connected Yugria with the 10th–11th century ethnic name Ugur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Novgorod reached the Ob by two routes from Ustyug, one by the Sukhona, Vychegda and Usa, the other along the coasts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a> and Kara Sea, and traded iron goods and textiles for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>. Yugorshchina, a trade association, was set up in Novgorod in the 14th century. Tribute was collected only by force, and chronicles record defeats, such as the campaign led by Yadrei of Novgorod in 1193. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> annexed Novgorod, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> sent expeditions in 1483 and 1499–1500. The 4,000-strong force of 1499, using dog and reindeer teams, is said to have taken 40 strongholds and captured 58 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanty">Khanty</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansi">Mansi</a> princes.</p><p>The Yugrans formed three or four proto-states, among them the Principality of Pelym and the largely Mansi Principality of Konda. Allied with the Siberia Khanate, the Mansi of Pelym raided Kaigorod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherdyn">Cherdyn</a> in 1581, and the campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yermak-timofeyevich">Yermak Timofeyevich</a> in 1582–1584 followed. In 1593 the stronghold of Prince Ablegirim of Pelym was taken and a Russian fortress built in it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> subdued Yugra only in the middle of the 17th century. The Mansi were baptised in numbers from the early 18th century, when Grigory Novitsky recorded the resistance of the village elder Nahratch Yeplayev; many sacred objects were later burned, though others were hidden.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The name survives in Khanty-Mansi Autonomous Okrug–Yugra, a constituent of the Russian Federation, and Yugoriya serves as a poetic name for the region. Yugra and the lands to its south are regarded as the place of origin of the Hungarians, whose language is the closest relative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanty">Khanty</a> and Mansi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yugra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…with honey, wax, hides and slaves, were brought from the northern peoples whom Arab writers called the Wisu and the <b>Yura</b>, and from the Rus, and were exchanged at Bolghar for silver coins, textiles, glass and other goods from Khwarazm, the Samanid lands of Central Asia and Iran.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
